  "image_name": "463ae86ef70f226220ca20f4fad7824c.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several ways to build mixed donor–acceptor morphologies in polymer-based electronic films and how polymer fibrils crystallize during drying. At the top, four schematic patterns compare phase arrangements: a simple two-phase network, a cascade-like distribution with small inclusions, a more uniformly mixed alloy, and a parallel structure where the two components form adjacent, partially aligned pathways.\n\nThe middle row translates these ideas to actual materials. One schematic shows a blend containing polymer fibrils and molecular domains, with labels such as PC71BM, BTR, and NITI, plus a magnified view suggesting separated electron and hole pathways around the fibrillar network. Another shows interpenetrating acceptor and donor fibrils, with a zoom-in indicating charge separation along contacting fibrils and labels including L8-BO, PM6, and D18.\n\nThe bottom row focuses on crystallization. A graph indicates that low-boiling solvents drive rapid, nucleation-mediated crystallization early in drying, whereas additives or thermal/solvent-vapor annealing extend drying and promote grain-boundary-induced crystallization. The accompanying mechanism diagram shows polymer chains first forming nuclei, then ordered fibrils, followed by additional surface crystallization that thickens or extends the fibrillar crystalline structure.",
